WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.928
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:11.910
LEWIS TUFF: So I'm Lewis Tuff,
head of platform at Blockchain.

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:14.040
So today I'm going to
be talking about how

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:17.470
we brought retail trading to 34
million cryptocurrency wallets

00:00:17.470 --> 00:00:17.970
globally.

00:00:21.290 --> 00:00:24.160
So first off, who is Blockchain?

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:27.740
Well, you may have heard of
the term brandied around.

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:30.600
So we are Blockchain.com,
a technology company,

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:35.720
and actually the world's
leading digital asset platform.

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:38.660
So we started our life
as a Blockchain explorer,

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:40.510
specifically for Bitcoin.

00:00:40.510 --> 00:00:42.890
And then we've evolved
and grown many products

00:00:42.890 --> 00:00:45.510
over the last five years.

00:00:45.510 --> 00:00:48.740
One of our most popular is
our cryptocurrency wallet.

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:51.150
We've also launched a number
of our consumer products,

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:54.150
including the ability
to trade cryptocurrency.

00:00:54.150 --> 00:00:57.640
And we have data
products, market research,

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:01.265
APIs for live streaming data
on Blockchain analytics.

00:01:01.265 --> 00:01:03.820
And we've also launched a number
of institutional products.

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:05.900
I'll go into more
details on some of these

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:07.246
later on in the presentation.

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:14.540
So how do we compare
to the market?

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:17.280
And who is our main competition?

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:20.090
So you may have heard of
a company called Coinbase.

00:01:20.090 --> 00:01:22.340
So the difference
between us and them

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:26.180
is that they've focused more on
the custodial offering, which

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:30.780
means they are taking custody of
your digital assets and funds.

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:33.230
And in return, you can use
their platform and services.

00:01:33.230 --> 00:01:36.870
For us, we've taken a
non-custodial first approach,

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:38.300
which means we're
empowering users

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:40.790
to take full control
of their private keys

00:01:40.790 --> 00:01:42.140
and digital assets.

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:44.810
At no point do we take
ownership of those

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:46.745
and compromise your
security or privacy.

00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:54.015
So that means you are no longer
reliant on a third party,

00:01:54.015 --> 00:02:00.110
a centralized entity, or
risk of any one person losing

00:02:00.110 --> 00:02:04.850
or being compromised with
your digital assets and funds.

00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:07.190
So our key aim really is to
empower users and educate

00:02:07.190 --> 00:02:09.259
them to allow them
to take control

00:02:09.259 --> 00:02:12.510
in a secure and simple manner.

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:15.560
And our tagline here, you
can see, is be your own bank.

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:19.150
We want every user to consider
becoming their own bank.

00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:21.750
And that is surprisingly
easier said than done.

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:27.870
So we have three main clients.

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:32.460
We have an iOS, an Android,
and a web platform.

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:36.350
Here are some screenshots
of our iOS application.

00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:39.530
And I've highlighted some of the
key features and functionality

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:43.200
that our mobile
applications offer.

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:45.350
As I mentioned, these
are all non-custodial.

00:02:45.350 --> 00:02:47.610
So their fat
clients, or wallets.

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:50.840
So a lot of the heavy lifting
is done actually browser side,

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:53.010
or on the client themselves.

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:55.580
Our backend is purely
to store some metadata

00:02:55.580 --> 00:03:00.810
and to interact with the
underlying Blockchain network.

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:02.720
So here you can see
you have an overview

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:05.030
of your cryptocurrency assets--

00:03:05.030 --> 00:03:07.630
your current balances,
the live market price.

00:03:07.630 --> 00:03:10.490
You have the ability to buy and
sell cryptocurrencies with fiat

00:03:10.490 --> 00:03:11.787
on and off ramps.

00:03:11.787 --> 00:03:13.370
You can exchange
your cryptocurrencies

00:03:13.370 --> 00:03:15.670
for any other asset you
may be interested in

00:03:15.670 --> 00:03:16.610
and get live pricing.

00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:18.860
And I'll go into a bit more
detail on the architecture

00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:20.780
behind that.

00:03:20.780 --> 00:03:23.270
And then finally,
a common use case,

00:03:23.270 --> 00:03:26.420
you can send and receive
cryptocurrency funds

00:03:26.420 --> 00:03:29.060
very easily and very
securely from right

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:33.380
within our mobile apps or
from within the wallet.

00:03:33.380 --> 00:03:36.000
And as I say, you never have to
give up custody of your assets

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:36.500
to do this.

00:03:41.590 --> 00:03:43.610
So where are we today?

00:03:43.610 --> 00:03:48.280
So right now, we have 34 million
wallets globally and growing.

00:03:48.280 --> 00:03:51.370
We're doing over 10 million
new wallets a year, currently.

00:03:51.370 --> 00:03:53.500
That equates to around
100,000 transactions

00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:58.780
per day that are being processed
through our software platform.

00:03:58.780 --> 00:04:01.210
In total to date,
that's $200 billion

00:04:01.210 --> 00:04:04.330
worth of transacted volume has
flowed through our software

00:04:04.330 --> 00:04:06.550
application and platform.

00:04:06.550 --> 00:04:08.140
And I think, for
me, most excitingly,

00:04:08.140 --> 00:04:11.320
that actually accounts for
25% of the global network

00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:15.260
traffic on the Bitcoin network.

00:04:15.260 --> 00:04:17.260
And then finally,
we currently have

00:04:17.260 --> 00:04:21.461
a user base that spans over 140
different countries worldwide.

00:04:25.790 --> 00:04:29.000
So first up, our Explorer--

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:31.660
so here on the
slide, you can see

00:04:31.660 --> 00:04:35.860
we have this common interface
into many different underlying

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:39.460
blockchains, notably Bitcoin,
Ethereum, and Bitcoin

00:04:39.460 --> 00:04:41.050
Cash right now.

00:04:41.050 --> 00:04:42.885
There's three main
sections on the explorer.

00:04:42.885 --> 00:04:44.260
The first is the
input to look up

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:48.100
your address, your
transaction hash, or a block.

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:52.600
The second middle section is
key stats and blockchain metrics

00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:56.390
such as the current difficulty
or the mempool size.

00:04:56.390 --> 00:04:57.970
And then finally,
we have a real time

00:04:57.970 --> 00:05:00.370
view of incoming
transactions and blocks

00:05:00.370 --> 00:05:02.680
that are being mined.

00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:04.180
So I want to delve
now a bit deeper

00:05:04.180 --> 00:05:08.980
into what does it take to
run a web platform like that?

00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:12.910
And how do we achieve, in
real time, the stream of data

00:05:12.910 --> 00:05:15.250
from these underlying chains?

00:05:15.250 --> 00:05:17.520
And more importantly, how
do we do that at scale?

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:19.645
If we're getting around 2
and 1/2 thousand requests

00:05:19.645 --> 00:05:22.510
per minute consistently,
and that kind of peaks

00:05:22.510 --> 00:05:25.885
even higher with
significant price movements.

00:05:30.070 --> 00:05:32.010
So how does it work?

00:05:32.010 --> 00:05:35.040
So this is obviously
a simplified overview.

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:37.290
But as I mentioned, we
have three main consumers

00:05:37.290 --> 00:05:38.830
of our platform.

00:05:38.830 --> 00:05:42.540
So that is our mobile wallets
on iOS, Android, and the web.

00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:44.520
We have our Explorer,
which I just showed you

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:45.790
on the previous slide.

00:05:45.790 --> 00:05:47.490
And we also have a
number of public APIs

00:05:47.490 --> 00:05:49.590
where any individual
or company can

00:05:49.590 --> 00:05:52.940
build on top of our platform.

00:05:52.940 --> 00:05:55.990
And they can actually use it for
creating transactions, sending,

00:05:55.990 --> 00:05:58.950
receiving, and also just
consuming and adjusting

00:05:58.950 --> 00:06:00.867
the blockchain data
directly without having

00:06:00.867 --> 00:06:03.450
to do the kind of hard work of
integrating with the underlying

00:06:03.450 --> 00:06:05.280
chains.

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:07.530
And in the middle, we have
this data ingestion service

00:06:07.530 --> 00:06:08.388
platform.

00:06:08.388 --> 00:06:09.930
So internal to us,
we've [? bought ?]

00:06:09.930 --> 00:06:12.720
out a number of services that
are responsible for interacting

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:16.140
with underlying chains such
as Bitcoin, Ethereum, Cash,

00:06:16.140 --> 00:06:17.580
Stellar, et cetera.

00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:19.590
So we create a
wrapper around these

00:06:19.590 --> 00:06:23.700
that interacts with the low
level APIs that they provide.

00:06:23.700 --> 00:06:27.400
We actually spin up and run
all of our own nodes in GCP.

00:06:27.400 --> 00:06:29.820
And then we have a number
of our servicing components

00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:34.320
that then ingest all of the real
time blocks and transactions,

00:06:34.320 --> 00:06:37.230
extract the key
[INAUDIBLE] and metrics,

00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:39.190
do any kind of
transformations we need,

00:06:39.190 --> 00:06:42.660
and persist that within
a relational database.

00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:49.410
We then serve that back up to
our clients on demand at scale.

00:06:49.410 --> 00:06:51.270
Between that, we do
have caching layers

00:06:51.270 --> 00:06:53.940
and a number of
other technologies

00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:58.310
to mitigate issues at scale,
which I will go into a bit

00:06:58.310 --> 00:06:58.810
further.

00:07:01.730 --> 00:07:06.190
The key thing here is that a
lot of the complexity of pulling

00:07:06.190 --> 00:07:08.860
out a very simple
attribute like your balance

00:07:08.860 --> 00:07:12.520
is abstracted away from the
user, both in terms of the UI

00:07:12.520 --> 00:07:14.980
and UX experience you get
from within our clients,

00:07:14.980 --> 00:07:16.950
but also for our unified API.

00:07:16.950 --> 00:07:19.660
So you no longer have
to spin up your node,

00:07:19.660 --> 00:07:21.340
become a part of
the public network,

00:07:21.340 --> 00:07:25.360
understand their APIs, which
are often pretty low level,

00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:29.800
interpret the data, which
again is usually at a lower

00:07:29.800 --> 00:07:32.090
level of abstraction.

00:07:32.090 --> 00:07:34.120
Now you just have one
API to interact with

00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:37.260
to seamlessly send,
receive, and process

00:07:37.260 --> 00:07:38.260
all the blockchain data.

00:07:41.950 --> 00:07:43.565
So how did we evolve?

00:07:43.565 --> 00:07:44.940
So I wanted to
just set the scene

00:07:44.940 --> 00:07:46.357
on the scale of
our infrastructure

00:07:46.357 --> 00:07:49.820
to support our platform,
both the Explorer,

00:07:49.820 --> 00:07:52.905
cryptocurrency wallet, and
a number of other products.

00:07:52.905 --> 00:07:54.905
So you can see here we're
running over 2 and 1/2

00:07:54.905 --> 00:07:57.720
thousand cores currently
in GCP and around nine

00:07:57.720 --> 00:08:00.490
terabytes of virtual memory.

00:08:00.490 --> 00:08:02.100
You can see the graph.

00:08:02.100 --> 00:08:04.350
The wallet accounts
that have been created

00:08:04.350 --> 00:08:07.350
over the last five, six years
have grown tremendously,

00:08:07.350 --> 00:08:09.630
especially since
2017, when a news

00:08:09.630 --> 00:08:12.030
kind of hit mainstream,
and cryptocurrencies

00:08:12.030 --> 00:08:14.357
were suddenly being discussed
by every single person

00:08:14.357 --> 00:08:14.940
on the street.

00:08:17.930 --> 00:08:19.950
I think, astonishingly,
and something

00:08:19.950 --> 00:08:22.620
that we realized
wasn't going to scale

00:08:22.620 --> 00:08:24.570
was that a third of
our infrastructure

00:08:24.570 --> 00:08:29.940
was dedicated to our
in memory NDB cluster.

00:08:29.940 --> 00:08:32.395
And this was
running our Explorer

00:08:32.395 --> 00:08:33.895
and our cryptocurrency
wallets, sort

00:08:33.895 --> 00:08:36.450
of the backbone for all those.

00:08:36.450 --> 00:08:41.010
And it was done ourselves
by our engineering team.

00:08:41.010 --> 00:08:44.710
So we were using, most
predominantly, Compute Engine

00:08:44.710 --> 00:08:48.528
and running all this
stuff ourselves at scale.

00:08:48.528 --> 00:08:50.820
As many of you will know that
doing that beyond a point

00:08:50.820 --> 00:08:53.940
becomes extremely costly, but
from a monetary perspective,

00:08:53.940 --> 00:08:56.370
but also from the operational
overhead of maintaining

00:08:56.370 --> 00:09:01.090
it, upgrading it, and dealing
with the fallout of failures.

00:09:01.090 --> 00:09:03.408
So we ran over 24 nodes to
bake in our own redundancy

00:09:03.408 --> 00:09:04.950
and make sure we
were fault tolerant.

00:09:08.640 --> 00:09:11.760
And to do any kind
of scaling, we

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:14.970
could not scale
linearly or on demand.

00:09:14.970 --> 00:09:18.360
We had to actually spin
up a whole new cluster

00:09:18.360 --> 00:09:22.200
alongside our
production cluster,

00:09:22.200 --> 00:09:25.600
restore each of the
underlying chains--

00:09:25.600 --> 00:09:27.480
for example, the
Bitcoin blockchain--

00:09:27.480 --> 00:09:30.280
from the beginning.

00:09:30.280 --> 00:09:32.610
And that would take around
one week, all being well.

00:09:34.868 --> 00:09:37.410
And then once it had caught up,
we could then flip the switch

00:09:37.410 --> 00:09:41.460
and move our applications over
to the new production cluster.

00:09:41.460 --> 00:09:44.400
Because of the pain involved
and the time it took to do this,

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:48.660
we would just-- last year just
doubled our infrastructure

00:09:48.660 --> 00:09:51.510
overnight just to support
our exponential growth.

00:09:54.380 --> 00:09:58.755
Now it's quickly realized
that this is not sustainable.

00:09:58.755 --> 00:10:00.130
So the beginning
of last year, we

00:10:00.130 --> 00:10:03.540
started to consider what are
the other options we have here?

00:10:03.540 --> 00:10:06.460
And how can we reduce
the operational overhead

00:10:06.460 --> 00:10:09.370
of running this at
scale and ensuring

00:10:09.370 --> 00:10:12.880
that our infrastructure
was going to support us

00:10:12.880 --> 00:10:17.110
on our mission to
grow and redefine

00:10:17.110 --> 00:10:18.860
the financial
infrastructure of the world?

00:10:22.890 --> 00:10:25.522
So in comes Cloud Spanner.

00:10:25.522 --> 00:10:27.480
So we looked at a number
of different products.

00:10:30.140 --> 00:10:32.560
And we decided to try a
prototype and [? POC ?]

00:10:32.560 --> 00:10:34.350
on Cloud Spanner.

00:10:34.350 --> 00:10:37.060
Now the impressive thing here
is that within a few minutes

00:10:37.060 --> 00:10:39.700
we were able to spin up
the production cluster

00:10:39.700 --> 00:10:46.150
and have strong consistency,
globally distributed, at scale,

00:10:46.150 --> 00:10:49.575
and all of that for a
marginal increase in cost

00:10:49.575 --> 00:10:50.950
as to running just
Compute Engine

00:10:50.950 --> 00:10:53.750
and managing this
infrastructure ourselves.

00:10:53.750 --> 00:10:56.860
So the early signs
were promising.

00:10:56.860 --> 00:10:59.920
We then switched and
re-architected our application,

00:10:59.920 --> 00:11:02.970
started to reinvent
our schema, and then

00:11:02.970 --> 00:11:06.730
started to actually go all in
on Cloud Spanner, initially

00:11:06.730 --> 00:11:09.070
for our Bitcoin blockchain
ingestion process.

00:11:11.590 --> 00:11:13.310
One of the key things
we did early on,

00:11:13.310 --> 00:11:15.670
which really benefited
the engineering team,

00:11:15.670 --> 00:11:19.727
was that we connected with the
Cloud Spanner team directly.

00:11:19.727 --> 00:11:22.060
So we had a few conversations
with technical [INAUDIBLE]

00:11:22.060 --> 00:11:24.257
and a number of
engineers in her team.

00:11:24.257 --> 00:11:26.590
And they've really helped us
through the initial hurdles

00:11:26.590 --> 00:11:29.140
of getting set up on
a brand new technology

00:11:29.140 --> 00:11:31.450
and really understanding
the nuances of where

00:11:31.450 --> 00:11:34.180
it was going to work and some
of the constraints of running

00:11:34.180 --> 00:11:35.740
at scale.

00:11:35.740 --> 00:11:38.890
We had to make some
design decisions

00:11:38.890 --> 00:11:41.530
early on that without
that help would've

00:11:41.530 --> 00:11:45.490
been costly to change later.

00:11:45.490 --> 00:11:48.820
I think one of the key features
that really benefited us

00:11:48.820 --> 00:11:51.220
during this iterative
development process

00:11:51.220 --> 00:11:53.875
was the fact that we
can now do a full import

00:11:53.875 --> 00:11:55.740
and export in record time.

00:11:55.740 --> 00:12:01.060
So we could back up our whole
database in just 30 minutes.

00:12:01.060 --> 00:12:03.700
As I mentioned before, it
took one week to restore that.

00:12:03.700 --> 00:12:05.760
We can now do it in a
batch job overnights,

00:12:05.760 --> 00:12:08.020
in just 8 and 1/2 hours.

00:12:08.020 --> 00:12:10.900
So what this meant is
that we could rapidly

00:12:10.900 --> 00:12:14.982
develop our application and
services, do a lot of testing

00:12:14.982 --> 00:12:16.690
and playing around
with different schemas

00:12:16.690 --> 00:12:19.265
and architectures, and
also, once we were happy,

00:12:19.265 --> 00:12:20.890
we could then promote
that through each

00:12:20.890 --> 00:12:23.000
of the environments--
dev, staging, pre-prod,

00:12:23.000 --> 00:12:25.030
and then finally, to production.

00:12:25.030 --> 00:12:28.560
And that was simple,
quick, and efficient.

00:12:28.560 --> 00:12:32.110
You'll notice here, as
well, that we have--

00:12:32.110 --> 00:12:34.300
this is our
production comparison.

00:12:34.300 --> 00:12:36.820
So we have under half
of the original nodes

00:12:36.820 --> 00:12:38.953
that we needed before,
now just 10 nodes.

00:12:38.953 --> 00:12:40.870
And all the reasons for
that is because you're

00:12:40.870 --> 00:12:43.060
getting redundancy,
high availability,

00:12:43.060 --> 00:12:46.197
strong consistency,
and global replication

00:12:46.197 --> 00:12:47.155
as part of the package.

00:12:49.678 --> 00:12:51.470
And then finally, we
could scale on demand.

00:12:51.470 --> 00:12:53.230
So at the touch of
a button, we could

00:12:53.230 --> 00:12:55.235
double our infrastructure.

00:12:55.235 --> 00:12:56.860
We could also set
predefined thresholds

00:12:56.860 --> 00:12:58.920
and have that elastic on demand.

00:13:02.900 --> 00:13:06.838
Final thing is we actually
leveraged table interleaving,

00:13:06.838 --> 00:13:09.380
which meant that we could take
some of the complex queries we

00:13:09.380 --> 00:13:11.840
were running against our
NDB cluster previously

00:13:11.840 --> 00:13:15.110
and reduce the time it took to
run those from 30 seconds down

00:13:15.110 --> 00:13:18.540
to around 15 milliseconds.

00:13:18.540 --> 00:13:20.570
So the whole migration
was hugely impactful

00:13:20.570 --> 00:13:23.570
for us and our products.

00:13:23.570 --> 00:13:26.270
And now we've been running
this for around six months

00:13:26.270 --> 00:13:28.040
in production.

00:13:28.040 --> 00:13:30.800
Overall, on average-- I just
did a comparison for our team--

00:13:30.800 --> 00:13:33.950
and we are saving
around 30% month

00:13:33.950 --> 00:13:36.850
on month in terms of
the dollar amount, which

00:13:36.850 --> 00:13:40.190
is pretty impressive when
usually some of hesitation

00:13:40.190 --> 00:13:44.450
about moving to software as a
service and some of these cloud

00:13:44.450 --> 00:13:47.337
services is the expense is going
to just creep up as you grow.

00:13:47.337 --> 00:13:48.920
But actually, we've
seen the opposite.

00:13:48.920 --> 00:13:51.020
We've not only reduced
the operational overhead

00:13:51.020 --> 00:13:52.400
of managing this to scale.

00:13:52.400 --> 00:13:54.890
We've also made
our CFO very happy.

00:13:59.070 --> 00:14:02.850
So how else is
this dataset used?

00:14:02.850 --> 00:14:06.152
And what other
products do we offer?

00:14:06.152 --> 00:14:07.610
So as I mentioned
earlier, we built

00:14:07.610 --> 00:14:11.540
out a crypto to crypto
exchange product,

00:14:11.540 --> 00:14:14.660
again, leveraging a
number of cloud services

00:14:14.660 --> 00:14:16.570
and operating at a
significant scale.

00:14:19.230 --> 00:14:24.150
So this allows you to exchange
across chain transactions

00:14:24.150 --> 00:14:26.310
and get live
exchange-like pricing

00:14:26.310 --> 00:14:29.580
right from within your
non-custodial wallet.

00:14:29.580 --> 00:14:34.590
So again, you never have to send
your funds anywhere and give up

00:14:34.590 --> 00:14:38.070
the custody of your assets to
any one individual, company,

00:14:38.070 --> 00:14:39.690
or entity.

00:14:39.690 --> 00:14:41.928
Your private keys remain
with you on your device

00:14:41.928 --> 00:14:42.720
or in your browser.

00:14:45.330 --> 00:14:47.440
Here's a quick demo
of the product.

00:14:47.440 --> 00:14:49.410
So this is on one of
our mobile applications.

00:14:49.410 --> 00:14:53.040
You'd simply just type in the
amount you wish to sell or buy.

00:14:53.040 --> 00:14:56.550
You'd get a live pricing
right from within the app

00:14:56.550 --> 00:14:58.290
in real time.

00:14:58.290 --> 00:15:01.420
And you can exchange between
multiple pairs that we offer.

00:15:01.420 --> 00:15:06.147
You finally have a summary
screen, Confirm, and that's it.

00:15:06.147 --> 00:15:07.230
So what's really going on?

00:15:07.230 --> 00:15:08.647
And what are some
the complexities

00:15:08.647 --> 00:15:10.620
with building out this--

00:15:10.620 --> 00:15:13.475
hopefully you'd agree--
smooth user experience?

00:15:16.450 --> 00:15:18.260
So the key challenges--

00:15:18.260 --> 00:15:22.040
security-- so for us, Blockchain
as a company, security

00:15:22.040 --> 00:15:26.480
is our number one priority
in everything we do.

00:15:26.480 --> 00:15:28.790
You may have heard of
some of the horror stories

00:15:28.790 --> 00:15:31.520
from cryptocurrency
companies in the space that

00:15:31.520 --> 00:15:35.900
have been hacked, compromised,
or lost their user's funds.

00:15:35.900 --> 00:15:37.490
We don't want be
the next headline.

00:15:37.490 --> 00:15:39.865
So everything we do goes
through a very rigorous process.

00:15:42.110 --> 00:15:44.600
Volatility-- in the
marketplace, if any of you

00:15:44.600 --> 00:15:47.030
follow the
cryptocurrency pricing,

00:15:47.030 --> 00:15:49.820
you'll see that it's extremely
volatile, maybe swinging

00:15:49.820 --> 00:15:52.630
by 20% to 50% in a day.

00:15:52.630 --> 00:15:55.910
So how do you create a
smooth user experience

00:15:55.910 --> 00:15:59.580
when the price is jumping
around all over the place?

00:15:59.580 --> 00:16:01.970
The third challenge--
public network--

00:16:01.970 --> 00:16:04.110
I think this is
an important one.

00:16:04.110 --> 00:16:06.290
Now we're relying
on infrastructure

00:16:06.290 --> 00:16:08.750
that is outside of our control.

00:16:08.750 --> 00:16:13.730
We're no longer just relying on
our private network within GCP

00:16:13.730 --> 00:16:15.320
and the services
that we've built.

00:16:15.320 --> 00:16:16.860
We now have to
broadcast and send

00:16:16.860 --> 00:16:20.720
transactions over a network
and many nodes globally

00:16:20.720 --> 00:16:22.910
distributed.

00:16:22.910 --> 00:16:26.160
So what are some of the
constraints when you do that?

00:16:26.160 --> 00:16:27.780
And finally, high availability--

00:16:27.780 --> 00:16:31.250
I think it's kind of a given
for any technology company now.

00:16:31.250 --> 00:16:33.870
But it's worth noting, how
do you achieve that at scale?

00:16:33.870 --> 00:16:35.870
And again, what are some
of the design decisions

00:16:35.870 --> 00:16:37.328
and architectural
decision you have

00:16:37.328 --> 00:16:39.320
to make early on to
ensure you achieve that?

00:16:42.390 --> 00:16:45.870
So the interesting
part-- how does it work?

00:16:45.870 --> 00:16:48.230
So I wanted to give you a
little bit of information

00:16:48.230 --> 00:16:51.140
on what's under the hood,
and how does the architecture

00:16:51.140 --> 00:16:52.068
actually look?

00:16:52.068 --> 00:16:54.110
This is a slight
simplification, but it gives you

00:16:54.110 --> 00:16:56.760
a very good idea about
some of the core components

00:16:56.760 --> 00:16:58.910
and interactions between those.

00:16:58.910 --> 00:17:02.310
We do leverage a number of
cloud services to achieve this.

00:17:02.310 --> 00:17:04.310
And I'll mention where
they fit into this stack.

00:17:07.369 --> 00:17:11.050
But as you saw in the
previous slide, first of all,

00:17:11.050 --> 00:17:15.615
we start with a client,
so Android, iOS, or web.

00:17:15.615 --> 00:17:16.990
You then have a
secure connection

00:17:16.990 --> 00:17:20.200
to our backend of
our client gateways.

00:17:20.200 --> 00:17:23.890
And the user will connect
to the swap product,

00:17:23.890 --> 00:17:26.369
initiate a secure
website connection,

00:17:26.369 --> 00:17:30.530
and this will start streaming
live prices to their device.

00:17:30.530 --> 00:17:34.600
The user types in the volume
they wish to sell or buy.

00:17:34.600 --> 00:17:38.920
And we then adjust our
pricing based upon that input.

00:17:38.920 --> 00:17:41.030
We have a liquidity
and execution engine,

00:17:41.030 --> 00:17:44.380
which is the driving force of
all of our trading activity.

00:17:44.380 --> 00:17:47.080
And that has connections to
many different exchanges,

00:17:47.080 --> 00:17:51.680
market makers, and brokers, and
also our internal inventory.

00:17:51.680 --> 00:17:53.890
So that's managing risk,
portfolio management,

00:17:53.890 --> 00:17:56.920
and pricing.

00:17:56.920 --> 00:17:58.780
Internally, as well,
we've also built

00:17:58.780 --> 00:18:00.370
our own custodial solution.

00:18:00.370 --> 00:18:03.040
This is a multi-tiered
custody solution

00:18:03.040 --> 00:18:06.890
on which the hot wallet is
at the top of the stack.

00:18:06.890 --> 00:18:10.240
So this is the only
part of it, or layer,

00:18:10.240 --> 00:18:13.090
that is programmatically
accessible and secured

00:18:13.090 --> 00:18:14.690
within our internal network.

00:18:14.690 --> 00:18:18.250
All other are air gapped and
have a number of other security

00:18:18.250 --> 00:18:21.500
policies in place.

00:18:21.500 --> 00:18:25.180
So once the user's happy with
the price, they hit exchange.

00:18:25.180 --> 00:18:26.753
What happens next?

00:18:26.753 --> 00:18:29.170
So our client gateways will
go off to our liquidity engine

00:18:29.170 --> 00:18:30.878
and say, hey, I want
to execute the trade

00:18:30.878 --> 00:18:34.070
to sell half a bitcoin.

00:18:34.070 --> 00:18:36.070
The liquidity engine with
then lock in the price

00:18:36.070 --> 00:18:39.940
and send back how much
of the counter currency

00:18:39.940 --> 00:18:41.420
they wish to receive.

00:18:41.420 --> 00:18:43.180
So let's, for
example, say I want

00:18:43.180 --> 00:18:44.620
to sell Bitcoin for Ethereum.

00:18:47.200 --> 00:18:49.080
In parallel, we
initiate requests

00:18:49.080 --> 00:18:55.430
to our hot wallet, which is the
gateway to all of our nodes.

00:18:55.430 --> 00:18:59.460
So these are the blockchain
nodes, Stellar, XLM, Bitcoin,

00:18:59.460 --> 00:19:03.230
Bitcoin Cash,
Ethereum, et cetera.

00:19:03.230 --> 00:19:05.650
We'll then generate a deposit
address which the hot wallet

00:19:05.650 --> 00:19:08.650
service will persist
and start listening on

00:19:08.650 --> 00:19:10.090
for any incoming deposits.

00:19:10.090 --> 00:19:11.590
We think combine
these transactions,

00:19:11.590 --> 00:19:15.610
send it back to the
client, and the client then

00:19:15.610 --> 00:19:18.490
returns to the user
the final amount

00:19:18.490 --> 00:19:23.380
they need to send us to
receive their Ethereum.

00:19:23.380 --> 00:19:26.140
And on the client's
side, the browser

00:19:26.140 --> 00:19:30.340
and/or the mobile client will
construct that transaction

00:19:30.340 --> 00:19:33.480
and sign it using
their private keys

00:19:33.480 --> 00:19:36.280
and then broadcast that out
to the underlying respective

00:19:36.280 --> 00:19:37.995
network.

00:19:37.995 --> 00:19:39.370
None of that
touches our backend.

00:19:39.370 --> 00:19:41.537
That's all done client side,
as I mentioned earlier.

00:19:44.070 --> 00:19:45.920
Now it will be
sent and broadcast

00:19:45.920 --> 00:19:47.490
across the public network.

00:19:47.490 --> 00:19:52.640
And we wait patiently for that
to hit a network broadcast

00:19:52.640 --> 00:19:54.800
event and a confirmation event.

00:19:54.800 --> 00:19:57.410
And the hot wallet is
responsible for that.

00:19:57.410 --> 00:20:00.450
Once those have been received
off the public network,

00:20:00.450 --> 00:20:03.410
we then use Kafka as
our main message bus.

00:20:03.410 --> 00:20:05.520
So we have an event
driven architecture.

00:20:05.520 --> 00:20:07.997
So this sends an
event over Kafka.

00:20:07.997 --> 00:20:09.830
One of the consumers
is our client gateways.

00:20:09.830 --> 00:20:13.383
That picks it up and
then processes that.

00:20:13.383 --> 00:20:15.800
We then compare that against
our state machine, transition

00:20:15.800 --> 00:20:18.950
through the lifecycle,
say, hey, deposit received.

00:20:18.950 --> 00:20:22.850
We now need to
pend a withdrawal.

00:20:22.850 --> 00:20:25.550
We update the user and say, hey,
we've received your deposit.

00:20:25.550 --> 00:20:28.008
We're now going to initiate
the withdrawal request for you.

00:20:28.008 --> 00:20:30.770
We then make a call
back to the hot wallet,

00:20:30.770 --> 00:20:33.590
and send the amount
we wish to withdraw

00:20:33.590 --> 00:20:36.892
and the deposit address that was
sent from a client originally.

00:20:36.892 --> 00:20:39.350
And again, that's then broadcast
out on the public network.

00:20:42.880 --> 00:20:46.130
The client will then receive
the funds in due course,

00:20:46.130 --> 00:20:48.400
usually within half an hour.

00:20:48.400 --> 00:20:51.340
And we notify them via
push, via email, via SMS.

00:20:55.750 --> 00:20:58.930
I think one of the interesting
cases that happened recently

00:20:58.930 --> 00:21:01.430
is that these public blockchains
can have many different use

00:21:01.430 --> 00:21:06.460
cases apart from just
transferring a store of value.

00:21:06.460 --> 00:21:10.230
And one of them is actually
being leveraged for marketing.

00:21:10.230 --> 00:21:12.710
So what that means is
actually every time anyone

00:21:12.710 --> 00:21:15.650
sends a transaction on
the Bitcoin Cash network,

00:21:15.650 --> 00:21:17.150
if someone is
listening, because you

00:21:17.150 --> 00:21:18.525
have this public
ledger-- they're

00:21:18.525 --> 00:21:19.905
listening for this address.

00:21:19.905 --> 00:21:21.530
Any new address that
appears, they then

00:21:21.530 --> 00:21:24.902
send you a little
marketing message.

00:21:24.902 --> 00:21:26.360
And what does that
mean materially?

00:21:26.360 --> 00:21:29.210
It means that actually
we, as a company

00:21:29.210 --> 00:21:32.000
on our [INAUDIBLE]
address receive more funds

00:21:32.000 --> 00:21:33.890
than the user actually sent.

00:21:33.890 --> 00:21:36.730
And also, the user receives
slightly more funds

00:21:36.730 --> 00:21:38.840
than we send them.

00:21:38.840 --> 00:21:41.490
They also receive two
transactions instead of one.

00:21:41.490 --> 00:21:43.820
So this was kind of a
very interesting use case

00:21:43.820 --> 00:21:46.070
where we had to be
flexible and defensively

00:21:46.070 --> 00:21:50.310
program for this in the future.

00:21:50.310 --> 00:21:52.520
And finally, the node
gateways at the bottom

00:21:52.520 --> 00:21:54.680
are really the data
ingestion service platform

00:21:54.680 --> 00:21:58.490
that I mentioned previously,
and are our way of wrapping each

00:21:58.490 --> 00:22:00.290
of the underlying
blockchain nodes.

00:22:06.510 --> 00:22:09.580
So one of the other interesting
things that we've managed to do

00:22:09.580 --> 00:22:13.570
is build out a
hardware device which

00:22:13.570 --> 00:22:19.940
is a secure way of storing your
cryptocurrency assets offline.

00:22:19.940 --> 00:22:23.720
This has full
interoperability with all

00:22:23.720 --> 00:22:25.650
of our platform and software.

00:22:25.650 --> 00:22:29.375
So you can use our swap product,
as I have shown you before.

00:22:29.375 --> 00:22:31.625
And your keys never have to
leave the physical device.

00:22:34.690 --> 00:22:37.030
So this is hugely
beneficial to a lot

00:22:37.030 --> 00:22:39.150
of users who are
security conscious

00:22:39.150 --> 00:22:41.950
and maybe don't trust the
terminal or workstation they

00:22:41.950 --> 00:22:44.490
are using.

00:22:44.490 --> 00:22:49.840
And we've managed to achieve
this leveraging our existing

00:22:49.840 --> 00:22:51.130
interface and software.

00:22:57.570 --> 00:22:59.490
Some the key benefits
of using Google--

00:22:59.490 --> 00:23:01.420
so I mentioned a few
of these already.

00:23:01.420 --> 00:23:04.470
But I wanted to kind of
summarize and highlight these

00:23:04.470 --> 00:23:07.170
before finishing up.

00:23:07.170 --> 00:23:09.690
So one of the things that GCP
has really allowed us to do

00:23:09.690 --> 00:23:14.520
is scale up our
infrastructure on demand.

00:23:14.520 --> 00:23:17.340
When you're dealing with tens
of millions of wallet accounts

00:23:17.340 --> 00:23:20.610
globally, it's important
that you're not

00:23:20.610 --> 00:23:22.710
constrained by
the infrastructure

00:23:22.710 --> 00:23:25.770
that you are using.

00:23:25.770 --> 00:23:28.980
We've had many cases when the
price has moved significantly.

00:23:28.980 --> 00:23:32.610
And suddenly the number of users
that we're onboarding every day

00:23:32.610 --> 00:23:34.125
has gone up five or 10 times.

00:23:36.512 --> 00:23:38.720
And we've had to actually
scale up our infrastructure

00:23:38.720 --> 00:23:41.820
significantly during
those periods.

00:23:41.820 --> 00:23:45.270
I mean, in the peaks
of the last few months,

00:23:45.270 --> 00:23:47.450
we've seen over 100,000
new wallets being

00:23:47.450 --> 00:23:51.980
created every single day, and up
to one million in a given week.

00:23:54.750 --> 00:23:57.740
So there are significant
strains on our infrastructure

00:23:57.740 --> 00:24:00.010
that we have to account for.

00:24:00.010 --> 00:24:02.760
And one of the things we do
do, as I mentioned previously,

00:24:02.760 --> 00:24:05.750
is adding a number
of caching layers,

00:24:05.750 --> 00:24:10.490
take a lot of advantage of CDNs,
and do a lot of load balancing

00:24:10.490 --> 00:24:12.320
across many instances.

00:24:12.320 --> 00:24:14.737
For the most part, all of our
infrastructure is stateless.

00:24:14.737 --> 00:24:16.112
So that means we
can horizontally

00:24:16.112 --> 00:24:17.120
scale it on demand.

00:24:19.680 --> 00:24:22.170
Also, on the previous
architecture,

00:24:22.170 --> 00:24:25.435
we're leveraging cloud
services where possible.

00:24:25.435 --> 00:24:29.160
It means that our team
can focus and hone

00:24:29.160 --> 00:24:32.190
in on some of our
very specific domain

00:24:32.190 --> 00:24:34.260
complexities and
challenges and not

00:24:34.260 --> 00:24:36.750
worry about running our
infrastructure at scale

00:24:36.750 --> 00:24:38.250
and solving the
problems that Google

00:24:38.250 --> 00:24:39.580
have been doing for many years.

00:24:42.840 --> 00:24:45.300
So they spend less
time, energy, and effort

00:24:45.300 --> 00:24:48.120
on managing our
infrastructure and more time

00:24:48.120 --> 00:24:50.430
focusing on building
what we believe

00:24:50.430 --> 00:24:51.615
to be the future of finance.

00:24:55.900 --> 00:24:57.650
As I mentioned, we
have high availability.

00:24:57.650 --> 00:24:59.600
So we're leveraging Cloud SQL.

00:24:59.600 --> 00:25:01.760
We're leveraging Spanner.

00:25:01.760 --> 00:25:04.040
We used a number
of other services

00:25:04.040 --> 00:25:07.677
quite broadly across
our architecture.

00:25:07.677 --> 00:25:09.760
As you can imagine, we
have a lot of microservices

00:25:09.760 --> 00:25:11.420
within our architecture.

00:25:11.420 --> 00:25:13.360
So being able to
just quickly spin up

00:25:13.360 --> 00:25:18.460
a highly available, redundant,
and scalable database

00:25:18.460 --> 00:25:21.000
at a click of a button
is very powerful.

00:25:21.000 --> 00:25:24.610
And we're using both Postgres
and MySQL engines for that.

00:25:27.240 --> 00:25:31.350
And the third point is that
it's engineering friendly.

00:25:31.350 --> 00:25:34.030
I think all of our engineers,
whatever their skill set

00:25:34.030 --> 00:25:37.890
or focus, enjoy
the GCP experience,

00:25:37.890 --> 00:25:40.560
whether that's because
of using the CLI

00:25:40.560 --> 00:25:43.500
and pragmatically writing
scripts around that

00:25:43.500 --> 00:25:47.100
or using the interface
itself to dive into problems

00:25:47.100 --> 00:25:48.090
and debugging issues.

00:25:51.470 --> 00:25:53.720
And finally, the fourth one,
which I mentioned earlier

00:25:53.720 --> 00:25:57.540
with our Spanner story,
is the technical support.

00:25:57.540 --> 00:26:00.740
So I think a lot of people don't
realize the amount of support

00:26:00.740 --> 00:26:04.760
that Google offer
you as a company.

00:26:04.760 --> 00:26:08.630
We can tap into a wealth of
knowledge and domain expertise

00:26:08.630 --> 00:26:11.000
through our account managers
and customer engineers.

00:26:11.000 --> 00:26:14.390
And we do that pretty regularly.

00:26:14.390 --> 00:26:17.360
We've had many conversations
comparing Google products

00:26:17.360 --> 00:26:20.030
to other open source
technologies and the pros

00:26:20.030 --> 00:26:23.310
and cons of using them.

00:26:23.310 --> 00:26:26.420
So another story that
happened last year,

00:26:26.420 --> 00:26:28.940
we were just trying to decide
on the correct message bus

00:26:28.940 --> 00:26:33.300
architecture for this trading
product and for many others.

00:26:33.300 --> 00:26:36.470
One of our key requirements
was sequencing.

00:26:36.470 --> 00:26:39.440
So we then spoke with
the technical PMs

00:26:39.440 --> 00:26:42.770
from the Pub/Sub team and had
a real candid conversation

00:26:42.770 --> 00:26:45.800
about what were the
pros and cons of using

00:26:45.800 --> 00:26:49.245
Pub/Sub above Kafka.

00:26:49.245 --> 00:26:50.870
And the net result
of that conversation

00:26:50.870 --> 00:26:52.670
was that we used Kafka.

00:26:52.670 --> 00:26:54.290
And that was their
recommendation.

00:26:54.290 --> 00:26:55.790
I think that's quite
refreshing when

00:26:55.790 --> 00:26:58.790
you can actually connect
with the engineers at a cloud

00:26:58.790 --> 00:26:59.690
company.

00:26:59.690 --> 00:27:01.730
And they recommend a
product which they do not

00:27:01.730 --> 00:27:03.890
support or sell themselves.

00:27:03.890 --> 00:27:06.640
[MUSIC PLAYING]

